Synthetic S1 native record store -> bounded expert RAM cache bridge
=================================================================
Source-only CPU prototype until parent compilation/validation. No serving Engine,
GPU, learned model, checkpoint authentication, production payload or installation.
All prior cache/store/GPU folders and Radiance source remain unchanged.

What is actually connected
-------------------------
record_cache_bridge.{h,cpp} adapts the existing frozen S1 Reader to the existing
frozen expert_cache Provider/Cache. It does not parse the private S1 manifest or
pretend that an opaque offset is a raw checkpoint offset. The wrapper owns both
objects and does not expose an acquire path which bypasses freshness validation.

open(directory,Fresh,Limits,out) requires:
* FULL fresh S1 RecordSpecs and SourceCatalog plus the independently retained
  publication manifest checksum. S1 Reader::open validates exact spec/catalog,
  every native record checksum/padding, segments and synthetic provenance.
* Explicit world/rank binding. This test binds one rank of a synthetic TP4 store;
  no TP kernels or MTP draft lifecycle run. Full S1 specs must share that world;
  only the chosen rank's record refs are exposed to this cache.
* Bounded record/source/manifest/node/segment/descriptor counts and snapshot,
  metadata, cache and staging budgets, checked before publication.

S1 exposes no record checksum catalog. Rather than duplicate its private parser,
the adapter performs one sequential checked Reader::read per S1 record at startup,
including unbound ranks. Each temporary vector is released before the next read.
This derives checksums for bound-rank refs and detects normal mutation between
S1 descriptor binding and the bridge's independent watches. Startup payload reads
are explicit counters, not a HOT hit/miss statistic. In the synthetic fixture:
16startupreads,720896successful payload bytes, peak private vector57344 bytes.
S1 Reader::open also performs its own streaming validation/padding I/O; those
additional internal read calls are not exposed and are NOT included in this
startup/read counter. No disk bandwidth/cache benchmark claim is made.

Identity and immutable records
------------------------------
Each CacheRef contains:
 source_id: S1 source checksum + retained publication manifest checksum;
 model_id: explicit synthetic_not_checkpoint;
 layout_id: checksum of the full native RecordSpec metadata;
 record_class: distinct main_mxfp4_e8m0 or mtp_fp8_f32 synthetic class;
 world/rank/layer/expert, exact native stride and whole-record checksum.
The opaque offset field is a bridge catalog ordinal, NOT a segment/file offset.
Only exact refs from the privately retained catalog are accepted. Mutating rank,
world, class, source/layout, ordinal, length or checksum refuses the ENTIRE
request before cache acquisition/provider I/O. Main and MTP records can coexist
as distinct entries; changing one class into the other cannot alias an entry.

The caller supplies Fresh on EVERY acquire, including HOT. The bridge compares
all source fields, paths/checksums/encoding and complete public RecordSpec metadata
against its owned canonical length-prefixed snapshot. No caller pointer/reference
is retained after open. Snapshot comparisons stream fields directly against the
owned baseline; the HOT/request path does not allocate a second full snapshot.
Fresh order is intentionally strict: equivalent reordered spec/catalog lists
require reopening a new binding. Empty/oversized/unterminated metadata refuses.
S1 RecordSpecs are immutable captures; public metadata_json may allocate one
bounded captured-spec string during comparison. FNV1a64 is integrity/version
identification only, NOT cryptographic authentication or collision resistance.
Full fresh snapshot equality is also checked; identities are not hash-only guards.

Ordinary mutation detection and limits
-------------------------------------
The bridge pins additional manifest/segment descriptors and checks fd/path
(device,inode,size,mode,mtime,ctime), directory identity and file types before
and after acquire, even for HOT hits. Symlinks and replaced ordinary paths refuse.
This does not reread HOT payloads, so a HOT pass adds zero Provider::read payload
I/O. Source checkpoint files need not remain online after publication: the native
store and its independently retained source fingerprint define this immutable
version. Changing fresh source metadata refuses instead of silently rebinding.

STAT CHECKS ARE ORDINARY MUTATION DETECTION, NOT CONCURRENT-WRITER EXCLUSION OR
TAMPER-PROOF INTEGRITY. The store must be immutable while bound. Metadata checks
cannot eliminate all TOCTOU windows, malicious writers, privileged timestamp
manipulation or filesystem behavior. A miss still uses S1's pinned Reader and
whole-record checksum before copying into a cache buffer. Existing immutable HOT
leases retain their verified bytes after a later path/store refusal. The bridge
refuses future use of that binding; reopening with fresh validated inputs is the
recovery path. It does not mutate an existing binding to accept a new version.

Memory and exact copy accounting
-------------------------------
The unchanged S1 Reader::read returns one private vector. Provider fills are
serialized and reserve that record's full length under staging_bytes BEFORE
calling Reader. It verifies size/checksum/cancellation before one explicit copy
into the cache's already reserved COLD buffer. That temporary vector is charged
in separate staging usage/peak counters until destroyed. This is one bounded
extra vector and copy, NOT zero-copy or pinned memory. Cache cold/hot accounting
remains unchanged and never conceals the Reader temporary.

Default payload reservation: HOT90112 + COLD229376 + staging65536 =385024 bytes,
under an explicit16MiB configurable logical payload cap (absolute256MiB).
The fixture's HOT capacity holds one main32768+one MTP57344 record. Leases protect
both tiers from eviction and keep the actual cache ledger alive after the bridge
is destroyed. Destructor must not race active calls. Provider/Reader/watch
resources are destroyed only after Cache; outstanding payload leases need none
of those descriptors and remain readable/charged independently.

Setup metadata is separately bounded by manifest bytes/nodes, specs/sources,
cache catalog/index counts, snapshot bytes and watch descriptors. The default
logical metadata reservation is7799296 bytes, under8MiB. This includes a
conservative2snapshot allowance,128bytes per bounded manifest node,1024bytes
per source record/cache catalog slot and duplicated descriptor/name allowances.
It is a logical fixture reservation/count cap, not a malloc/RSS/allocator-overhead
measurement; STL/JSON node and allocator bookkeeping are not exact byte charges.
No payload-sized metadata vectors or duplicated private manifest JSON are used.
Both S1 and watch descriptor ownership are explicit: at most260open descriptors
under default segment cap128, actual28for12segments. Additional fresh metadata
may allocate bounded captured-spec JSON strings, not model payload buffers.

Atomicity, concurrency and failures
----------------------------------
open constructs everything privately and publishes *out only after all startup
validation/reads, identities, watches and Cache construction succeed. Acquires
serialize at the wrapper; the Cache's own serialized union admission and Provider
rules remain unchanged. Every ref is validated before cache acquisition. The
cache reserves the full missing union before the first Provider call; no expert
is dropped to make a budget fit. Provider runs no recursive cache mutations.

The wrapper acquires into a private Batch and swaps caller leases only after
post-checks and cancellation. Any refusal leaves the caller's existing Batch
unchanged; private ready/temporary leases release normally. A post-validation
failure is NOT rollback of internal cache fills: already verified immutable
entries may remain internally admitted, but no new caller Batch is published.
There is no cross-operator or filesystem transaction claim.

Tests use actual S1 pack/capture/Reader plus actual bounded Cache:
* Tiny main/MTP records from actual Builder/Planner/weight_share ROWparts2 GU and
  COL DN shares, all4ranks in the store; cache-bound rank0 only. Independent
  expected byte coordinates cover codes, scales, both ROW halves and all padding.
* Deduplicated mixed-class demand, exact HOT bytes/pointer reuse and zero repeat
  provider payload reads; a second pair while first stays leased; live HOT/COLD
  charges through trim and all payload leases after bridge destruction.
* Whole-batch cross-rank/class/layout/source/ref mutations and fresh catalog/
  publication/spec changes, including HOT, all before provider I/O/publication.
* Corrupt/truncated/replaced segments and changed manifest after HOT fill refuse;
  previous leases stay exact. A corrupt late native record prevents open output
  publication. This is not a deterministic concurrent mid-pread fault injection.
* Explicit payload/staging/metadata/FD/snapshot/rank caps, whole-union budget
  refusal before partial read, cancellation and staging refund.
* Eight concurrent callers,30acquires each, same main/MTP union and exact shared
  immutable leases; two provider reads only and one private staging vector.
* Test-only ordinary allocation fault replacement (thread_local, one failure at
  a time) checks open and sampled early/middle/late miss-acquire allocation
  failures preserve published objects/caller leases and refund staging/COLD.
  No runtime/store/cache hooks or sources are changed.

Build scope
-----------
Parent builds glm53_record_cache_bridge_test in a new external build directory.
CMake compiles unchanged frozen native_record_store.cpp/expert_cache.cpp alongside
the new adapter, permitting matching sanitizer instrumentation without invoking
or editing those folders' CMake. It links parent-selected existing HOST Core
archives for the real metadata fixture, no implicit Core build/device query.
No CTest automatic run, plugin registration, service change or model I/O exists.
Parent owns all compilers and execution; child produced source only.

Next separate gates
-------------------
Adapting this source to the validated tiny native GPU fixture; proving native
R4D Builder record capture at each actual class/shape; real checkpoint headers/
payload validation and source pipeline; runtime Engine ownership/route handoff;
large-model budgets/latency; TP coordinated preparation/failure agreement and
MTP draft/verify lifecycle. This CPU bridge does not establish any of them.
